//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fortifications.
//
// Rules from docs/usr/Defense.md. Baseline 6.2 asked for fixed positions and an
// upgrade button rather than free placement, and that document says what goes in
// the positions and what happens when a level lands.
//
// A LOCATION is a room or a corridor. The distinction is flavour -- both are
// regions with fortification positions in them and both take a level -- so
// nothing here branches on which kind it is.
//
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Money and time are two separate costs, and that is the whole mechanic.
//
// The upgrade completes instantly and the location stays at that level however
// many buildings are destroyed. What does NOT happen instantly is the buildings:
// worker SCVs have to walk over and put them up, and they repair and rebuild for
// free forever after. So paying is a decision about minerals and getting the
// benefit is a decision about how many workers you built ten minutes ago.
//
// Two rules bite together and produce something worth noticing: a level cannot
// be bought until the previous level is fully standing, and buildings under
// sustained attack never finish. So A LOCATION THAT IS BEING OVERRUN CANNOT BE
// REINFORCED. You have to win the fight first and buy the answer afterwards,
// which is the opposite of panic-buying and is deliberate.
//
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Free repair is free because the buildings cost nothing.
//
// SC2's Repair charges a fraction of the unit's own Cost, so "SCVs rebuild at no
// charge" is a data decision rather than a script: give the fortification
// buildings Cost 0 and take the minerals on the upgrade instead. Repair and
// rebuild are then free with nothing written.
//
// The same choice makes construction free too, which is why a building is put up
// at a sliver of life and left to the workers. That is not a simulation of
// construction, it IS the construction: build time becomes repair time, repair
// time falls with the number of workers, and "更多的scv让修建和修复都更快" comes
// out without a build-time constant existing anywhere.
//
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The editor side: place the REAL unit, then it is read and deleted.
//
//     region  room_* / corridor_*     a location
//     group   fort1 / fort2 / fort3   a position, at that level
//
// Put the actual Lob_Turret_Basic or Lob_Guard_Marine exactly where it should
// stand and add it to the group for its level. At init this reads its type,
// position, facing and owner, then removes it. Building it later re-creates the
// same unit, in the same spot, facing the same way.
//
// This replaced a marker scheme -- Lob_Fort_L2_Guard_Marine parsed apart to mean
// "level 2, becomes Lob_Guard_Marine" -- and it is better on every count. There
// is no parallel marker unit to author, no string parsing, no naming convention
// to remember or mistype. Most of all it is WYSIWYG: a marker cannot tell you
// whether the turret actually FITS, and a turret placed in the editor is checked
// by the editor's own placement rules for free. Facing comes along for nothing,
// which a marker would have needed another channel for.
//
// The level moves to the editor group, which is a channel this project already
// has and which is preplaced-only -- exactly what a fixed position is.
//
// REMOVED rather than hidden, and that is the one part worth arguing. Hiding is
// possible: c_unitStateHidden is writable, as are Selectable, Targetable and
// Paused. But a hidden unit still occupies its footprint, so a level-3 turret
// emplacement would block its corridor from minute zero, and there is no unit
// state that turns pathing off. Hiding also means four flags that all have to be
// right, where each one forgotten is a bug that looks like something else --
// stray splash killing an invisible turret, a selection box catching nothing.
// Reading four fields and deleting the unit has none of that, and the editor
// still shows exactly what will be there.
//
// Whether a position is occupied is asked SPATIALLY -- is there a unit of that
// type standing on the spot -- so nothing bookkeeps which building belongs to
// which slot, and a building that dies simply makes its slot empty again.
//
// Anything whose type falls under Lob_Guard_* is garrison and warps in; anything
// else is a building and gets built. That is the only thing the distinction is
// for.
//
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Positions are indexed once. Enemies arrive on an event.
//
// Positions cannot move -- they are gone from the map after init -- so their
// location, level and product are settled once and never asked again.
//
// Enemy presence is the opposite kind of fact: it changes constantly and is
// almost always "no", so it is an event rather than a poll.
// TriggerAddEventUnitRegion on each location fires when something walks in, and
// the garrison is roused from there. Polling every location every second to
// discover that nothing has happened is the same waste in the other direction.
//
// What is left on a timer is only "an idle guard has drifted off its post",
// which is genuinely periodic and iterates the indexed slots rather than the map.
//
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Garrison arrives once per level, and death is permanent until the next one.
//
// Warping in happens the FIRST time a level's buildings all stand, and upgrading
// also tops up the dead from earlier levels. Rebuilding after a massacre does
// not. So a location whose garrison is wiped stays weaker until somebody spends
// money on it, or until a production building in the room or Central Command's
// passive replaces them -- neither of which exists yet, and both are noted where
// they go.
//
// That makes losing garrison genuinely cost something, which a free respawn
// would not.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const int c_fortMaxLevel = 3;

// TUNING, all of it. Minerals for each step up. Nobody has designed an economy
// curve and these are placeholders that make the mechanic visible.
int[4] gvg_fortCost;

const fixed c_fortTick = 2.0;

// How close a unit has to be to a position to count as standing on it.
const fixed c_fortSlotRadius = 1.5;

// How close a worker has to get before the building goes up.
const fixed c_fortBuildRange = 2.5;

// And how far an enemy has to be for the position to be usable at all. An
// occupied slot is refused rather than contested: docs/usr/Defense.md has the
// worker cancel and go do something else, which a command queue then covers.
const fixed c_fortEnemyRadius = 4.0;

// What a building starts at, as a percent of its maximum. The rest is the
// workers' job.
const fixed c_fortStartLifePct = 8.0;

// How far a garrison unit may drift from its post before being sent back.
const fixed c_fortPostSlack = 2.0;

// Editor groups: fort1, fort2, fort3.
const string c_fortGroupPrefix = "fort";

const int c_locMax = 32;
const int c_slotMax = 128;

int gv_locCount;
string[32] gvg_locName;
region[32] gvg_locRegion;
point[32] gvg_locCenter;
int[32] gvg_locLevel;

// Whether this location's level has ever stood complete, flattened
// location * 4 + level. What makes garrison a one-time arrival.
bool[128] gvg_locSeeded;

// Every fortification position on the map, read off the preplaced units before
// they were removed.
int gv_slotCount;
string[128] gvg_slotType;
point[128] gvg_slotAt;
fixed[128] gvg_slotFacing;
int[128] gvg_slotLoc;
int[128] gvg_slotLevel;
int[128] gvg_slotOwner;
bool[128] gvg_slotGuard;

trigger gt_fortTick;
trigger gt_fortEnter;

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Locations.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
int Fort_LocOfPoint (point where) {
    int i;

    i = 0;
    while (i < gv_locCount) {
        if (RegionContainsPoint(gvg_locRegion[i], where)) {
            return i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

int Fort_LocByName (string name) {
    int i;

    i = 0;
    while (i < gv_locCount) {
        if (gvg_locName[i] == name) {
            return i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

int Fort_LevelOf (int loc) {
    if (loc < 0 || loc >= gv_locCount) {
        return 0;
    }
    return gvg_locLevel[loc];
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Whose location this is: the same spatial question the containment cells ask,
// answered the same way.
//
// The region's centre rather than anything that walks into it, because this has
// to be answerable for an EMPTY room -- who may upgrade it, and who the
// buildings will belong to, are both questions asked before anybody is there.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
int Fort_OwnerOf (int loc) {
    if (loc < 0 || loc >= gv_locCount) {
        return 0;
    }
    return Sector_OwnerNear(gvg_locCenter[loc]);
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Levels bought across everything this player owns, added up.
//
// Safety Team 1 is "升级4个（累积等级）房间或走廊" -- four levels wherever they
// sit, so one corridor at 3 and one room at 1 is the same as four rooms at 1.
// The parenthesis in the doc is doing the work: it says accumulated LEVELS, not
// four separate places.
//
// Reads the level rather than counting standing buildings, because the level is
// what was paid for. A location whose emplacements have just been flattened has
// not un-bought them, and the design already says the level never falls.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
int Fort_LevelSumOf (int player) {
    int total;
    int loc;

    if (!Slot_IsTablePlayer(player)) {
        return 0;
    }

    total = 0;
    loc = 0;
    while (loc < gv_locCount) {
        if (Fort_OwnerOf(loc) == player) {
            total = total + gvg_locLevel[loc];
        }
        loc = loc + 1;
    }
    return total;
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Whose a position's occupant should be. The editor's own answer first, so
// placing an emplacement under a specific player overrides the geometry.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
int Fort_SlotOwner (int slot) {
    if (slot < 0 || slot >= gv_slotCount) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (Slot_IsTablePlayer(gvg_slotOwner[slot])) {
        return gvg_slotOwner[slot];
    }
    return Fort_OwnerOf(gvg_slotLoc[slot]);
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Positions, and whether they are occupied.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
unit Fort_OccupantOfSlot (int slot) {
    return UnitGroupClosestToPoint(
        UnitGroup(gvg_slotType[slot], c_playerAny,
                  RegionCircle(gvg_slotAt[slot], c_fortSlotRadius), null, 0),
        gvg_slotAt[slot]);
}

bool Fort_SlotFilled (int slot) {
    return Fort_OccupantOfSlot(slot) != null;
}

bool Fort_IsEnemyOf (unit who, int owner) {
    unitgroup one;

    if (who == null || owner < 1 || owner > 15) {
        return false;
    }
    one = UnitGroupEmpty();
    UnitGroupAdd(one, who);
    return UnitGroupCount(UnitGroupFilterAlliance(one, owner, c_unitAllianceEnemy, 0),
                          c_unitCountAlive) > 0;
}

bool Fort_SlotBlocked (int slot, int owner) {
    if (owner < 1 || owner > 15) {
        return false;
    }
    // Enemies only. An ally standing on the spot is not an obstacle, because the
    // building is created ignoring placement -- which is exactly the doc's
    // "friendly in the way means build anyway".
    return UnitCountAlliance(owner, c_unitAllianceEnemy,
                             RegionCircle(gvg_slotAt[slot], c_fortEnemyRadius),
                             null, c_unitCountAlive) > 0;
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Whether every building up to a level is standing. Buildings only -- garrison
// arrives because the buildings are done, so waiting on it would deadlock.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
bool Fort_BuildingsComplete (int loc, int level) {
    int slot;

    slot = 0;
    while (slot < gv_slotCount) {
        if (gvg_slotLoc[slot] == loc && !gvg_slotGuard[slot]
            && gvg_slotLevel[slot] <= level && !Fort_SlotFilled(slot)) {
            return false;
        }
        slot = slot + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Puts one position's occupant back on the map, facing the way it was placed.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
unit Fort_Raise (int slot, fixed lifePct) {
    unit made;
    int owner;

    owner = Fort_SlotOwner(slot);
    if (owner < 1 || owner > 15) {
        return null;
    }

    UnitCreate(1, gvg_slotType[slot], c_unitCreateIgnorePlacement, owner,
               gvg_slotAt[slot], gvg_slotFacing[slot]);
    made = UnitLastCreated();
    if (made != null && lifePct > 0.0 && lifePct < 100.0) {
        UnitSetPropertyFixed(made, c_unitPropLifePercent, lifePct);
    }
    return made;
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Garrison arrival.
//
// `upTo` is inclusive. `replaceOnly` skips levels that have never landed, so
// upgrading tops up what is missing without pre-delivering the level being
// bought -- that one waits for its buildings.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
void Fort_SeedGarrison (int loc, int upTo, bool replaceOnly) {
    int slot;
    int lvl;

    slot = 0;
    while (slot < gv_slotCount) {
        lvl = gvg_slotLevel[slot];
        if (gvg_slotLoc[slot] == loc && gvg_slotGuard[slot] && lvl <= upTo
            && (!replaceOnly || gvg_locSeeded[loc * 4 + lvl])
            && !Fort_SlotFilled(slot)) {
            Fort_Raise(slot, 100.0);
        }
        slot = slot + 1;
    }

    if (!replaceOnly) {
        lvl = 1;
        while (lvl <= upTo) {
            gvg_locSeeded[loc * 4 + lvl] = true;
            lvl = lvl + 1;
        }
    }
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Upgrading.
//
// A reason rather than a bare false, the same as Work_Refusal: a button that
// does nothing is the same bug for a player and for whoever has to debug it.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
string Fort_UpgradeRefusal (int player, int loc) {
    if (loc < 0 || loc >= gv_locCount) {
        return "no such location";
    }
    if (!Slot_IsTablePlayer(player)) {
        return "not a player";
    }
    if (gvg_locLevel[loc] >= c_fortMaxLevel) {
        return "already at maximum";
    }
    if (!Fort_BuildingsComplete(loc, gvg_locLevel[loc])) {
        return "the current level is not finished being built";
    }
    if (PlayerGetPropertyInt(player, c_playerPropMinerals)
        < gvg_fortCost[gvg_locLevel[loc] + 1]) {
        return "not enough minerals";
    }
    // Cross-department permission is Control Team's business and lives in
    // usr/department.md: lv1 allows other sectors to level 1, lv2 to level 3,
    // lv3 and lv4 without limit. There is no upgrade tree in the map yet, so
    // nothing is refused here rather than something being invented.
    return "";
}

bool Fort_Upgrade (int player, int loc) {
    int cost;

    if (Fort_UpgradeRefusal(player, loc) != "") {
        return false;
    }

    cost = gvg_fortCost[gvg_locLevel[loc] + 1];
    PlayerModifyPropertyInt(player, c_playerPropMinerals, c_playerPropOperSubtract, cost);
    gvg_locLevel[loc] = gvg_locLevel[loc] + 1;

    // Topping up earlier levels' dead garrison is part of buying a level, per
    // the doc. It happens now rather than when the new buildings finish, because
    // the earlier levels are already standing.
    Fort_SeedGarrison(loc, gvg_locLevel[loc] - 1, true);

    UIDisplayMessage(
        PlayerGroupAll(), c_messageAreaSubtitle,
        Msg2("Lob/Msg/FortLevel",
             "%PLACE%", StringToText(gvg_locName[loc]),
             "%LEVEL%", MsgInt(gvg_locLevel[loc])));
    return true;
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a worker should be doing about fortifications, or -1.
//
// Asked by 30_workers before it considers devices or debris, which is the
// ordering docs/usr/Defense.md gives: a worker clears debris when it has nothing
// else to do.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
int Fort_JobFor (unit worker) {
    point where;
    fixed bestDist;
    fixed d;
    int best;
    int slot;
    int loc;

    if (worker == null || !UnitIsAlive(worker)) {
        return -1;
    }
    where = UnitGetPosition(worker);
    best = -1;
    bestDist = 0.0;

    slot = 0;
    while (slot < gv_slotCount) {
        loc = gvg_slotLoc[slot];
        if (!gvg_slotGuard[slot] && gvg_slotLevel[slot] <= gvg_locLevel[loc]
            && !Fort_SlotFilled(slot) && !Fort_SlotBlocked(slot, Fort_SlotOwner(slot))) {
            d = DistanceBetweenPoints(gvg_slotAt[slot], where);
            if (best < 0 || d < bestDist) {
                best = slot;
                bestDist = d;
            }
        }
        slot = slot + 1;
    }
    return best;
}

point Fort_SlotPoint (int slot) {
    if (slot < 0 || slot >= gv_slotCount) {
        return Point(0.0, 0.0);
    }
    return gvg_slotAt[slot];
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A worker that has arrived puts the building up, at a sliver of life. Repair
// does the rest, which is what makes worker count the build speed.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
void Fort_RaiseIfArrived (unit worker, int slot) {
    if (slot < 0 || slot >= gv_slotCount || Fort_SlotFilled(slot)) {
        return;
    }
    if (DistanceBetweenPoints(UnitGetPosition(worker), gvg_slotAt[slot])
        > c_fortBuildRange) {
        return;
    }
    Fort_Raise(slot, c_fortStartLifePct);
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Damaged fortification buildings, so 30_workers can repair them alongside
// devices.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
unitgroup Fort_Damaged () {
    unitgroup found;
    unitgroup all;
    unit who;
    int count;
    int i;

    found = UnitGroupEmpty();
    all = ObjectsGen_ScanTurret();
    count = UnitGroupCount(all, c_unitCountAlive);
    i = 1;
    while (i <= count) {
        who = UnitGroupUnit(all, i);
        if (who != null && UnitIsAlive(who)
            && UnitGetPropertyFixed(who, c_unitPropLifePercent, true) < 100.0) {
            UnitGroupAdd(found, who);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return found;
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Something walked into a location.
//
// An event rather than a poll: enemy presence changes constantly and is almost
// always "no", so asking every location every second is a lot of work to learn
// nothing. This fires only when it is actually true.
//
// The whole garrison answers, which is the doc's rule -- one turret engaging
// pulls everybody at that location onto the same target. Read as "an enemy is
// here" rather than "a turret has a target", because a structure weapon firing
// on its own is not a thing script can reliably ask about.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
void Fort_Rouse (int loc, unit foe) {
    unit who;
    int slot;

    slot = 0;
    while (slot < gv_slotCount) {
        if (gvg_slotLoc[slot] == loc && gvg_slotGuard[slot]) {
            who = Fort_OccupantOfSlot(slot);
            if (who != null && UnitIsAlive(who)) {
                UnitIssueOrder(who, OrderTargetingUnit(AbilityCommand("attack", 0), foe),
                               c_orderQueueReplace);
            }
        }
        slot = slot + 1;
    }
}

bool Fort_Enter_Func (bool testConds, bool runActions) {
    unit who;
    int loc;

    if (!runActions) {
        return true;
    }

    who = EventUnit();
    if (who == null || !UnitIsAlive(who)) {
        return true;
    }

    loc = Fort_LocOfPoint(UnitGetPosition(who));
    if (loc >= 0 && Fort_IsEnemyOf(who, Fort_OwnerOf(loc))) {
        Fort_Rouse(loc, who);
    }
    return true;
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The only thing left that is genuinely periodic: a level that has just finished
// standing, and guards that have wandered off their posts.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
bool Fort_Tick_Func (bool testConds, bool runActions) {
    unit who;
    int loc;
    int slot;

    if (!runActions) {
        return true;
    }

    loc = 0;
    while (loc < gv_locCount) {
        // A level that has just finished standing for the first time delivers
        // its garrison. Rebuilding after a massacre does not come through here,
        // because the flag is already set.
        if (gvg_locLevel[loc] > 0
            && !gvg_locSeeded[loc * 4 + gvg_locLevel[loc]]
            && Fort_BuildingsComplete(loc, gvg_locLevel[loc])) {
            Fort_SeedGarrison(loc, gvg_locLevel[loc], false);
        }
        loc = loc + 1;
    }

    slot = 0;
    while (slot < gv_slotCount) {
        if (gvg_slotGuard[slot]) {
            who = Fort_OccupantOfSlot(slot);
            if (who != null && UnitOrderCount(who) == 0
                && DistanceBetweenPoints(UnitGetPosition(who), gvg_slotAt[slot])
                   > c_fortPostSlack) {
                UnitIssueOrder(who,
                               OrderTargetingPoint(AbilityCommand("move", 0), gvg_slotAt[slot]),
                               c_orderQueueReplace);
            }
        }
        slot = slot + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every room and corridor drawn in the editor, and every emplacement standing in
// one -- read off the real unit and then deleted.
//
// Everything is read BEFORE the removal, because after it the handle is a dead
// unit and every one of these questions answers wrong.
//--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
void Fort_ReadLevel (int level) {
    unitgroup placed;
    unit who;
    int count;
    int loc;
    int i;

    placed = ObjectsGen_Group(c_fortGroupPrefix + IntToString(level));
    count = UnitGroupCount(placed, c_unitCountAlive);

    i = 1;
    while (i <= count && gv_slotCount < c_slotMax) {
        who = UnitGroupUnit(placed, i);
        if (who != null) {
            loc = Fort_LocOfPoint(UnitGetPosition(who));
            if (loc >= 0) {
                gvg_slotType[gv_slotCount] = UnitGetType(who);
                gvg_slotAt[gv_slotCount] = UnitGetPosition(who);
                gvg_slotFacing[gv_slotCount] = UnitGetFacing(who);
                gvg_slotOwner[gv_slotCount] = UnitGetOwner(who);
                gvg_slotGuard[gv_slotCount] = ObjectsGen_IsGuard(who);
                gvg_slotLoc[gv_slotCount] = loc;
                gvg_slotLevel[gv_slotCount] = level;
                gv_slotCount = gv_slotCount + 1;
            }
            // Gone either way. An emplacement outside every location has no
            // level to belong to, and leaving it standing would be a free
            // turret nobody paid for.
            UnitRemove(who);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

void Fort_BuildRegistry () {
    string name;
    int i;

    gv_locCount = 0;
    i = 0;
    while (i < c_regionCount && gv_locCount < c_locMax) {
        name = gvg_regionNames[i];
        if (StringSub(name, 1, 5) == "room_" || StringSub(name, 1, 9) == "corridor_") {
            gvg_locName[gv_locCount] = name;
            gvg_locRegion[gv_locCount] = ObjectsGen_Region(name);
            gvg_locCenter[gv_locCount] = RegionGetCenter(gvg_locRegion[gv_locCount]);
            gvg_locLevel[gv_locCount] = 0;
            gv_locCount = gv_locCount + 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }

    gv_slotCount = 0;
    i = 1;
    while (i <= c_fortMaxLevel) {
        Fort_ReadLevel(i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

void Fortify_Init () {
    int i;

    gvg_fortCost[0] = 0;
    gvg_fortCost[1] = 150;
    gvg_fortCost[2] = 300;
    gvg_fortCost[3] = 600;

    i = 0;
    while (i < 128) {
        gvg_locSeeded[i] = false;
        i = i + 1;
    }

    Fort_BuildRegistry();

    gt_fortEnter = TriggerCreate("Fort_Enter_Func");
    i = 0;
    while (i < gv_locCount) {
        TriggerAddEventUnitRegion(gt_fortEnter, null, gvg_locRegion[i], true);
        i = i + 1;
    }

    gt_fortTick = TriggerCreate("Fort_Tick_Func");
    TriggerAddEventTimePeriodic(gt_fortTick, c_fortTick, c_timeGame);
}
